#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Run the fleet cleanup loop in its own context, and hand SIANA back only the
questions it has to answer.

Cleanup is long, repetitive and almost entirely mechanical, and it is exactly the
shape of work that eats a SIANA session: thirty worktrees enumerated, twenty-eight
of them retired without incident, and two that need a judgment. Done in SIANA's own
context that is thirty rounds of tool output crowding out the fleet; done here it is
two questions and a report.

This is the mechanics of that arrangement and none of its judgment. It starts one
cleanup agent, gives it a named grant, keeps its state on disk, and provides the
protocol by which it stops and asks. It never decides what to clean up, and it never
performs a cleanup itself: every destructive mechanic in this fleet already lives
behind a command that fails closed, and the whole point of delegating is that those
commands keep their judgments rather than having them copied in here.

**The authority boundary is SIANA, and nothing here moves it.** The cleaner may do
exactly what its grant names and nothing else. A question it cannot settle stops the
run. A question that belongs to the captain stops the run too, and SIANA turns that
one into an ordinary recorded decision with `siana-owe decision`: an answer given
here can never manufacture captain authority, because nothing here can close an
obligation, and `answer` refuses a captain question until an obligation id is named.

**The protocol cannot deadlock, because the child is gone before the question is
read.** A nested one-shot agent is one-way: it cannot call back into a parent that is
synchronously waiting on it, and a design that tried would hang both. So an uncertain
cleaner writes its question down and *ends*. The command returns to SIANA with exit
3, SIANA reads the question, records an answer, and `resume` starts a fresh child
carrying the durable state forward. Asking and resuming are two operations with the
process boundary between them, and there is no point at which either side waits on
the other.

**The question is on disk before anybody sees it.** `ask` writes it and returns; the
cleaner is only then permitted to stop. So a machine that dies between the two loses
a round of work and never loses the question.

**And nothing after the uncertain point runs**, which is enforced rather than asked
for: every shim in the guard below refuses while that question file is there. A
cleaner that ignored its instructions and kept going is stopped by the same mechanism
that keeps it inside its grant, because "no step after the uncertain point" is an
exact rule and an exact rule belongs in a script.

This command is itself in that guard, allowed `ask` and `runbook` and nothing else. A
cleaner that could answer its own question would remove the file every other shim
gates on, put its own words in the runbook as the fleet's policy, and then do the
thing it had just asked about, with SIANA never seeing the question.

Usage:
    siana-clean start [--project <handle>] [--grant <name>]...  begin one run
    siana-clean status [<run>] [--json]                         what a run is doing
    siana-clean answer <run> --text <answer>                    answer its question
    siana-clean resume <run>                                    carry on from there
    siana-clean abort <run> --reason <text>                     stop one, keep state
    siana-clean runbook                                         durable gotchas
    siana-clean ask --run <run> --body <text> --kind <kind>     the cleaner's own

Exit codes, in the shape `siana-pipeline` reads:

    0   the round finished and there is nothing outstanding
    1   refused, a fault, or a run deliberately aborted. Nothing was left half-done
        either way; the message says which it was and what to do about it
    3   a question is pending. Read it, answer it, resume

The grants, and there are four. They are named after the commands they unlock,
because a grant that does not name a command is a grant nobody can check:

    inventory        read the queue, the registry, git and herdr. Change nothing
    retire           also `siana-retire <task-id>`, one task at a time
    reap-report      also `siana-reap <project>` in its report-only form
    close-workspace  also `siana-close-workspace <task-id>`, after that task's
                     `siana-retire` succeeded and never before

`inventory` is always in force and is the default. There is no grant that reaches
`siana-reap --yes`: reaping is the one mistake in this fleet that loses work, so it
stays the captain's, and what a cleanup run produces about it is a report SIANA turns
into a decision.

**`close-workspace` is a whole grant of its own rather than part of `retire`,
because closing a workspace kills the agent in it.** The captain granted it narrowly
and deliberately, and the narrowness is in the command: `siana-close-workspace` takes
a task id, resolves the workspace from that task's recorded owner pane and from
nothing else, and refuses unless herdr agrees the workspace is a linked worktree one,
open on exactly the tree that task recorded, in exactly the repository the registry
gives that task's project, unfocused, with no other task's pane in it, and with its
agent in one of the states a finished minion leaves rather than one this fleet would
be interrupting.

Two orderings are load-bearing and both are refusals in that command rather than
instructions here. Closing a workspace before its worktree is removed strands the
worktree, so the retirement's postcondition - the tree gone from disk and gone from
git's own list - is checked before anything closes. And closing a project's *source*
workspace closes every worktree workspace under it, so a workspace herdr does not
mark `is_linked_worktree` is refused outright.

A run may hold `retire` and `close-workspace` together, which is the pairing the
whole thing is for: retire the tree, then close the workspace the retirement left
open. `retire` on its own is unchanged and closes nothing.

**What the guard is, and what it is not.** Every run puts a directory of refusing
shims on the front of the child's `PATH`, so a fleet command outside the grant fails
with a message naming what to do instead rather than running. That is a real, tested
boundary against the ordinary way an agent goes wrong, which is reaching for the next
obvious command. It is not a sandbox: an agent invoking `/usr/bin/git` by absolute
path would be outside it. What actually holds is that the destructive mechanics live
in commands that fail closed on their own inputs; the guard is what keeps the cleaner
from reaching for them by mistake, and what makes that fact testable.

The same caveat covers the runbook, and it is worth stating rather than leaving to be
discovered. Nothing but `answer` *writes* it, so a guess cannot be recorded through
this command - but the cleaner runs with a shell, and a shell redirection reaches any
file its user can write. The child is started without pi's `write` and `edit` tools,
because it has no use for either and they are the two that reach that file most
easily. That narrows the opening; it does not close it, and the cleaner's own
instructions carry the rule the way the grant does.
"""

import argparse
import json
import os
import re
import shutil
import signal
import subprocess
import sys
import threading
from datetime import UTC, datetime

# One line of the child's JSON stream can be a whole file the cleaner read, so the
# stream is capped rather than trusted. A cleanup round that has produced a megabyte
# of events has already gone wrong, and the useful part of it is the beginning.
STREAM_CAP = 1024 * 1024
# What of a round survives into the next one. The report is the cleaner's own summary
# and is what `resume` carries forward, so it is bounded to something a later prompt
# can hold: past this the round is quoting rather than reporting.
REPORT_CAP = 16 * 1024
# How long one round may run before it is killed. Cleanup is enumerate-and-delegate,
# so a round still going after half an hour is wedged rather than thorough.
ROUND_S = int(os.environ.get("SIANA_CLEAN_ROUND_S", "1800"))
# Between SIGTERM to the child's process group and SIGKILL. Long enough for pi to
# flush its last event, short enough that an abort is not itself a wait.
GRACE_S = 5

GRANTS = ("inventory", "retire", "reap-report", "close-workspace")

# What each shim refuses, and the whole of it. A command absent from this table is a
# command the child reaches unchanged, which is the right default: the cleaner has to
# read the world, and a guard that had to enumerate every reader would go stale
# against the first new one.
#
# Each entry is a tuple of clauses, and a command is refused when any of them fires.
# Two rather than one is what `siana-reap` needs and is why this is a list: it is
# refused outright to a run without the `reap-report` grant, *and* refused its `--yes`
# to a run that has it. With one clause it had only the second, so the grant unlocked
# nothing while four documents said it did.
#
# The word rules are matched over the child's whole argument list, because `git
# worktree remove` and `git -C x worktree remove` are the same call and only the
# words tell them apart.
GUARDS = {
    "siana-clean": (("only:ask,runbook",
                     "a cleaner asks; it never answers, starts, resumes or aborts"),),
    "siana-retire": (("grant:retire",
                      "this run's grant does not include retiring"),),
    "siana-close-workspace": (("grant:close-workspace",
                               "this run's grant does not include closing a"
                               " workspace"),),
    "siana-reap": (("grant:reap-report",
                    "this run's grant does not include reaping, even to report"),
                   ("globs:--y*,-y*",
                    "reaping is the captain's; report it and stop")),
    "siana-publish": (("always", "publishing is SIANA's, after QA accepts"),),
    "siana-dispatch": (("always", "dispatching is SIANA's"),),
    "siana-brief": (("always", "briefing is SIANA's"),),
    "siana-gate": (("always", "the gate is SIANA's"),),
    "siana-afk": (("always", "advisory sessions are the captain's"),),
    "siana-watch": (("always", "the watcher is the captain's grant"),),
    "siana-pipeline": (("always", "the pipeline validates a minion's branch"),),
    "siana-handoff": (("always", "a handoff is written by whoever did the work"),),
    "pi": (("always", "a cleanup run does not start another agent"),),
    # The cleaner's own rule is "you never remove a worktree yourself, with git or
    # with herdr or with `rm`", and two thirds of that was a mechanism while the
    # third was a sentence. The failure it leaves open is the one this design is
    # built against: a cleaner meets `siana-retire`'s refusal about a dirty or
    # unanchored tree and reaches for `rm -rf <worktree>`, which passes through none
    # of retire's checks and loses the work they were protecting. A run writes
    # nothing - it is started without the harness's write and edit tools for the same
    # reason - so `rm` has no use in one at all.
    "rm": (("always", "a cleanup run removes nothing itself; `siana-retire` does"),),
    "tasks": (("words:init,add,start,done,block,unblock,reset,dep,drop,"
               "setup,skill,pi-package",
               "the queue is SIANA's; a cleanup run reads it and never writes it"),),
    "datafile": (("words:put,delete,repair,compact,roll,setup,skill,pi-package",
                  "the stores are SIANA's; a cleanup run reads them and never"
                  " writes"),),
    "git": (("words:push,commit,merge,rebase,reset,clean,am,cherry-pick,revert,"
             "apply,restore,checkout,switch,stash,rm,mv",
             "a cleanup run changes neither a repository's history nor its"
             " working tree"),),
    # Named per operation, because after `close-workspace` landed there are two
    # commands here rather than one, and a refusal that points at the wrong one sends
    # a cleaner to a command that will not do what it was just refused. `siana-retire`
    # closes no workspace at all: it prints the close as a separate step and stops.
    "herdr": (("words:close,remove,kill,prompt",
               "removing a worktree is `siana-retire`'s and closing a workspace is"
               " `siana-close-workspace`'s; neither is done with herdr directly"),),
}


# The subcommands of a guarded command that only read, declared so that the ones that
# write can be checked against the command itself rather than against this file.
#
# A hand-written list of verbs drifts, and it drifts silently and in the dangerous
# direction. This one did: it named `delete`, `put`, `edit` and `set`, none of which
# `tasks` has, while missing `start`, `unblock`, `dep` and `init`, which it does -
# so `tasks start <id> --owner <name>` reached the real command and dispatched a task
# under the captain's queue, which is the one thing `siana-dispatch` is refused
# outright for. `datafile compact` and `datafile roll`, which rewrite an append-only
# store in place, were open for the same reason.
#
# `tests/test_clean.py` asks each command for its own verbs and refuses to pass while
# one of them is in neither list, so the next verb either of these grows is a red
# suite rather than a hole nobody sees.
GUARD_READS = {
    "tasks": ("show", "list"),
    "datafile": ("get", "list", "keys", "stores", "validate", "schema"),
}

# A word that is benign in one position and not in another, as {command: {word:
# flag}}: the word is refused everywhere except immediately after that flag.
#
# `tasks list --status done` is the whole reason this exists. It is the documented
# way to list finished tasks, it is the cleaner's primary enumeration - finished
# tasks are exactly the ones whose worktrees can be retired - and matching `done`
# anywhere in the argument list refused it while saying the cleaner had tried to
# write the queue.
#
# An exemption rather than a rule about where the subcommand sits, because both
# `tasks` and `datafile` take boolean global flags (`--ambient`, `--version`) and any
# positional rule that did not know about them would read the subcommand off by one
# and fail *open*. This fails closed: a word not in exactly the exempted position is
# still refused.
WORD_AFTER = {
    "tasks": {"done": "--status"},
}

# The commands the cleaner delegates to, which are themselves the safety boundary.
# They are exec'd with the guard taken off their PATH, because they have to reach the
# real tools to do their job: `siana-retire`'s last act is `git worktree remove`, and
# with the guard in front of it a cleanup run passed every one of retire's own checks
# and then died on that line. The guard exists to stop the *cleaner* reaching git
# directly, not to stop the command whose judgment the whole design defers to.
#
# `siana-close-workspace` is deliberately not here, although it is the same class of
# command. It reads git twice - `worktree list` and `rev-parse` - and the guard
# refuses neither, so an exemption would buy it nothing today and would widen the one
# opening this list is: a command exec'd past the guard is a command the guard is no
# longer watching. `tests/test_clean.py` drives it through the guard to a herdr read,
# which is past both git calls, so the day the guard grows a rule that does stand in
# its way is a red suite rather than a run that dies on its last line.
#
# Everything else keeps the guard, so a fleet command cannot become a way to launder
# an arbitrary call: these two take a task id and a project handle and nothing else.
DELEGATES = ("siana-retire", "siana-reap")

# `git worktree` and `git branch` read in their common form and destroy in their
# flagged one, so they are matched on the pair rather than on the verb. Kept apart
# from GUARDS because the rule is a different shape, and one table holding both would
# have to be read twice to be understood once.
GIT_PAIRS = (
    ("worktree", ("add", "remove", "prune", "move", "repair")),
    ("branch", ("-d", "-D", "-m", "-M", "--delete", "--move")),
    ("tag", ("-d", "--delete")),
)


class Refusal(SystemExit):
    """A refusal that still knows what it said, matching the rest of the fleet."""

    def __init__(self, message: str, hints=()):
        self.message, self.hints = message, tuple(hints)
        super().__init__(1)

    def __str__(self) -> str:
        return self.message


def die(message: str, *hints: str):
    raise Refusal(message, hints)


def now() -> str:
    return datetime.now(UTC).isoformat()


def duration(seconds: int) -> str:
    """A span in the units it actually has.

    The watchdog used to print `ROUND_S // 60` minutes. That reads correctly at the
    half-hour a round really gets and reads "0 minutes" at every value the suite
    drives it with, which is the one place the message is ever read closely. A
    watchdog naming a bound it did not enforce is the same defect as a refusal
    naming a mechanism that is not there.
    """
    minutes, rest = divmod(int(seconds), 60)
    parts = []
    if minutes:
        parts.append(f"{minutes} minute{'' if minutes == 1 else 's'}")
    if rest or not minutes:
        parts.append(f"{rest} second{'' if rest == 1 else 's'}")
    return " ".join(parts)


def process_command(pid: int) -> str:
    """What `ps` says this process is running, or "" when it says nothing.

    Identity, not liveness, for the reason `siana-watch` and `template/wake.ts` give:
    pids are reused, so a live pid is not a live child unless the process wearing it
    is still the one that was recorded.
    """
    try:
        return subprocess.run(["ps", "-p", str(pid), "-o", "command="],
                              capture_output=True, text=True,
                              timeout=10).stdout.strip()
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError, ValueError):
        return ""


def read_json(path: str, what: str):
    """A record this command wrote, or None when it has not written one.

    A file that will not parse is a stop and never a zero. Every write here lands by
    atomic rename, so an unreadable one was hand-edited or lost rather than caught
    half-written, and reading either as "no state" would let a run start on top of
    one already going.
    """
    try:
        with open(path) as fh:
            return json.load(fh)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return None
    except (OSError, json.JSONDecodeError) as exc:
        die(f"cannot read the {what} at {path}: {exc}",
            "it is written whole by an atomic rename, so an unreadable one was",
            "edited or lost rather than caught half-written",
            "move it aside; the run it belongs to is not resumable")


def write_json(path: str, record) -> None:
    """Beside the destination and then renamed, so a reader never catches a record
    half-written. A run's state is what says whether a destructive command may
    follow, and a torn read of that is the failure this whole design is built
    against."""
    tmp = f"{path}.{os.getpid()}"
    with open(tmp, "w") as fh:
        json.dump(record, fh, indent=2)
        fh.write("\n")
    os.replace(tmp, path)


class Home:
    """Where a cleanup run keeps everything, and the only place it does.

    One object rather than paths threaded through every function, because the
    invariant that matters is that no two of these ever disagree about where a run
    lives, and a constructor is the cheapest way to hold it.
    """

    def __init__(self, home: str):
        self.home = home
        self.root = os.path.join(home, "cleanup")
        self.runs = os.path.join(self.root, "runs")
        self.lock = os.path.join(self.root, "lock")
        self.runbook = os.path.join(home, "runbook.md")

    def run_dir(self, run_id: str) -> str:
        return os.path.join(self.runs, run_id)

    def record(self, run_id: str) -> str:
        return os.path.join(self.run_dir(run_id), "run.json")

    def question(self, run_id: str) -> str:
        return os.path.join(self.run_dir(run_id), "question.json")

    def answers(self, run_id: str) -> str:
        return os.path.join(self.run_dir(run_id), "answers.jsonl")

    def aborted(self, run_id: str) -> str:
        return os.path.join(self.run_dir(run_id), "aborted.json")

    def log(self, run_id: str) -> str:
        return os.path.join(self.run_dir(run_id), "rounds.jsonl")

    def stream(self, run_id: str, round_no: int) -> str:
        return os.path.join(self.run_dir(run_id), f"round-{round_no}.jsonl")

    def ids(self):
        """Every run there is, oldest first. The id sorts by its own clock, so this
        needs no stat and cannot be reordered by a touch."""
        try:
            return sorted(d for d in os.listdir(self.runs)
                          if os.path.isdir(os.path.join(self.runs, d)))
        except OSError:
            return []


def run_id_for(paths: Home) -> str:
    """The run id, and it is the clock rather than a counter.

    A counter needs somewhere to live and something to serialise its increment; a UTC
    second does not, sorts correctly in a directory listing, and says when the run
    happened in the one place the captain always sees it. Two runs in the same second
    are still two runs, so a taken id is suffixed rather than reused - the lock makes
    that a formality, and a formality is cheaper than a run silently joining another.
    """
    base = datetime.now(UTC).strftime("clean-%Y%m%d-%H%M%S")
    taken = set(paths.ids())
    if base not in taken:
        return base
    for n in range(2, 100):
        if f"{base}-{n}" not in taken:
            return f"{base}-{n}"
    die("cannot derive a free run id", "look at the cleanup runs directory by hand")


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The lock. One cleanup run at a time, and the refusal says which one.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

def read_lock(path: str):
    """Who holds the lock: a record, `{}` when the file names nobody, None when there
    is no file.

    Deliberately not `read_json`. That refusal is written for the records this
    command lands by atomic rename, and the lock is not one of them - it is the one
    file two racing processes are guaranteed to read at the same instant. An earlier
    version created it empty with `O_EXCL` and wrote the payload afterwards, so the
    loser of an ordinary race read the winner's empty file, was told the store was
    corrupt, and was advised to move a live run's lock aside. `take_lock` below
    creates it whole instead, so that window is gone; a lock that still will not
    parse was left by that older version or edited by hand, which is neither
    corruption to report nor a holder to overwrite.
    """
    try:
        with open(path) as fh:
            held = json.load(fh)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return None
    except (OSError, json.JSONDecodeError):
        return {}
    return held if isinstance(held, dict) else {}


def take_lock(paths: Home, what: str) -> None:
    """Exclusive, visible only whole, and reclaimed only from a process that is
    provably gone.

    `os.link` is both halves at once, which is why the payload is written to a
    temporary file first and linked into place after. The link is the exclusion -
    two `start`s racing reach it and exactly one of them creates the name - and it
    is also what makes the record and the file arrive together, so no observer can
    read a lock that does not yet say who holds it. A creator killed before the
    link leaves no lock at all rather than an empty one, so the next caller takes it
    without anything having to be deleted on a guess.

    A lock left by a killed process must not wedge the fleet forever, and a lock
    removed because a pid was reused would let two runs mutate one fleet, so
    liveness is the pid *and* what `ps` says it is running - the check `siana-watch`
    makes of its own grant, for the same reason.
    """
    os.makedirs(paths.root, exist_ok=True)
    record = {"pid": os.getpid(), "command": process_command(os.getpid()),
              "what": what, "taken": now()}
    tmp = f"{paths.lock}.{os.getpid()}"
    with open(tmp, "w") as fh:
        json.dump(record, fh)
    try:
        for attempt in (1, 2):
            try:
                os.link(tmp, paths.lock)
            except FileExistsError:
                held = read_lock(paths.lock)
                if held == {}:
                    # Nothing here can tell a live run from a leftover, because the
                    # file says nothing about either. Refused rather than reclaimed:
                    # deleting it on a guess is what the old corruption message
                    # advised, and it was advising the deletion of a live run's lock.
                    die(f"the cleanup lock at {paths.lock} does not name a holder",
                        "a lock is created whole, so this one was left by a version",
                        "that created it empty, or edited by hand",
                        "`siana-clean status` says whether a run is live; remove",
                        "the lock only if none is")
                if held is not None:
                    pid, command = held.get("pid"), held.get("command") or ""
                    if pid and command and process_command(pid) == command:
                        die("a cleanup run is already starting or resuming"
                            f" (pid {pid})",
                            f"it took the lock at {held.get('taken', '?')}"
                            f" to {held.get('what', '?')}",
                            "one at a time, so that two cleaners never mutate one"
                            " fleet",
                            "`siana-clean status` says what it is doing")
                if attempt == 2:
                    die("cannot take the cleanup lock",
                        f"{paths.lock} keeps coming back; look at it by hand")
                # Either the holder is gone - a leftover from a process killed
                # before its own release ran - or the lock was released between the
                # link and the read, and there is nothing left to remove. Removed
                # and retried once rather than forced: a second racer that took it
                # in between must win the retry.
                if held is not None:
                    try:
                        os.unlink(paths.lock)
                    except FileNotFoundError:
                        pass
                continue
            return
    finally:
        try:
            os.unlink(tmp)
        except FileNotFoundError:
            pass


def drop_lock(paths: Home) -> None:
    """Only ours. A lock reclaimed by a later run while this one was dying belongs to
    that run, and removing it here would leave that run unprotected."""
    held = read_lock(paths.lock) or {}
    if held.get("pid") == os.getpid():
        try:
            os.unlink(paths.lock)
        except FileNotFoundError:
            pass


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The guard. What a cleanup child may reach, as shims rather than as instructions.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

def clean_path() -> str:
    """The PATH without any guard directory on it.

    A resumed run builds a new guard, and looking the real command up through an
    older one would make each round's shim call the previous round's. Guards live
    under the cleanup root, so that prefix is the whole test.
    """
    home = os.environ.get("SIANA_HOME", "")
    path = os.environ.get("PATH", "")
    if not home:
        return path
    root = os.path.join(home, "cleanup")
    return os.pathsep.join(p for p in path.split(os.pathsep)
                           if not p.startswith(root))


def sq(text: str) -> str:
    """One shell word, single-quoted, so that nothing in it is ever interpreted.

    This is not tidiness. The refusal messages below are prose written for an agent
    to read, and prose contains backticks and apostrophes: `worktree removal is
    `siana-retire`'s` inside a double-quoted `echo` is a command substitution, and
    the first version of this file really did run `siana-retire` every time a
    cleaner asked herdr to close a workspace. A guard that executes its own refusal
    text is worse than no guard, so every string reaching a shim goes through here.
    """
    return "'" + text.replace("'", "'\\''") + "'"


def refusal_lines(name: str, instead: str):
    """What a shim says when it refuses.

    In the fleet's own shape, because the reader is an agent deciding what to do
    next, and a bare "permission denied" tells it to try something cleverer.
    """
    said = [f"refused: {name} is not this cleanup run's to call",
            f"  {instead}",
            "  stop and record what you found. siana-clean ask is how you put",
            "  a question in front of SIANA"]
    return [f"printf '%s\\n' {sq(line)} >&2" for line in said] + ["exit 1"]


def match_words(words, exempt=None, quote=True):
    """Shell that sets BAD when any argument is one of these words.

    `exempt` maps a word to the flag it is harmless directly after, so that a value
    is not mistaken for a subcommand. `quote` off leaves the entries as `sh` patterns
    rather than literals, which is how an option and its abbreviations are matched at
    once: argparse accepts `--ye` and `--y` for `--yes`, so a rule spelled out in
    exact strings is a rule with two spellings around it.
    """
    exempt = exempt or {}
    arms = []
    for word in words:
        pattern = sq(word) if quote else word
        if word in exempt:
            arms.append(f'    {pattern}) [ "$PREV" = {sq(exempt[word])} ] || BAD=1 ;;')
        else:
            arms.append(f"    {pattern}) BAD=1 ;;")
    return ['PREV=""', 'for a in "$@"; do', "  case \"$a\" in",
            "\n".join(arms), "  esac", '  PREV="$a"', "done"]


def match_first(allowed, name: str):
    """Shell that refuses unless the first argument is one of these.

    The inverse of every other rule here, and it is what `siana-clean` needs. That
    command is deliberately reachable from inside a run, because `ask` is how a
    cleaner puts a question in front of SIANA - but the same binary also carries
    `answer`, and a cleaner that answered its own question would remove the file
    every other shim gates on and then retire the tree it had just asked about,
    with SIANA never seeing the question. An allowlist is the only shape that says
    "this one verb and nothing else the binary happens to grow".
    """
    cases = "|".join(sq(a) for a in allowed)
    return [f'case "${{1:-}}" in', f"  {cases}) ;;", "  *) BAD=1 ;;", "esac"]


def match_pairs(verb, bad):
    """Shell that sets BAD when an argument follows `verb` and is one of `bad`.

    Positional rather than anywhere-in-the-line: `git worktree list` names a path
    that could itself be the word `remove`, and a rule that did not care where the
    word sat would refuse a reader for the name of a directory.
    """
    cases = "|".join(sq(b) for b in bad)
    return [
        "SAW=0",
        'for a in "$@"; do',
        '  if [ "$SAW" = 1 ]; then',
        '    case "$a" in',
        f"      {cases}) BAD=1 ;;",
        "    esac",
        "    SAW=0",
        "  fi",
        '  case "$a" in',
        f"    {sq(verb)}) SAW=1 ;;",
        "  esac",
        "done",
    ]


def question_gate(name: str, question: str):
    """Shell that refuses while this run has a question waiting.

    The guarantee is that nothing after the uncertain point runs until an answer is
    durably recorded, and until this existed the only thing holding it was a sentence
    in the cleaner's prompt. That is the wrong place for it by this project's own
    rule: an exact rule belongs in a script. The shims are written once at spawn
    time, so the check has to be at the front of the shim rather than baked into it,
    and the file it looks for is the same one `pending()` reads.

    Every guarded command gets this, readers included. After a cleaner has asked
    there is nothing further for it to do but finish its turn, so a reader that
    still answered would only be helping it gather more state it must not act on.
    """
    said = [f"refused: {name} is not callable while a question is waiting",
            "  you asked SIANA something and it has not been answered yet",
            "  stop now and finish your turn. This run is resumed with the answer"]
    return ([f'if [ -e {sq(question)} ]; then']
            + [f"  printf '%s\\n' {sq(line)} >&2" for line in said]
            + ["  exit 1", "fi"])


def shim(name: str, clauses, real, grants, question: str) -> str:
    """One shim's text: `sh`, because it runs on every command the child issues, and
    small enough that a reader can check it without trusting this comment.

    A clause is `always`, `grant:<name>`, `flags:<a,b>` or `words:<a,b>`. The first
    two are decided here, when the shim is written, because the grant does not change
    within a run; the last two are decided by the shim, because they are about the
    arguments it is given. Any clause firing refuses.
    """
    lines = ["#!/bin/sh", *question_gate(name, question)]
    for rule, instead in clauses:
        refuse = refusal_lines(name, instead)
        if rule == "always" or (rule.startswith("grant:")
                                and rule.split(":", 1)[1] not in grants):
            return "\n".join(["#!/bin/sh", *refuse]) + "\n"
        if real is None:
            # Nothing to fall through to. Written anyway rather than skipped: a
            # command absent today and installed tomorrow must not become reachable
            # to a run that was already refused it.
            return "\n".join(["#!/bin/sh", *refuse]) + "\n"
        if rule.startswith("grant:"):
            continue                       # granted, so this clause has nothing to say
        kind, values = rule.split(":", 1)
        if kind not in ("words", "flags", "globs", "only"):
            die(f"unknown guard rule: {rule}")
        lines.append("BAD=0")
        if kind == "only":
            lines += match_first(values.split(","), name)
        else:
            lines += match_words(values.split(","), WORD_AFTER.get(name),
                                 quote=kind != "globs")
        if kind == "words" and name == "git":
            for verb, bad in GIT_PAIRS:
                lines += match_pairs(verb, bad)
        lines += ['if [ "$BAD" = 1 ]; then',
                  *[f"  {line}" for line in refuse],
                  "fi"]
    if name in DELEGATES:
        lines.append(f'PATH={sq(clean_path())} exec {sq(real)} "$@"')
    else:
        lines.append(f'exec {sq(real)} "$@"')
    return "\n".join(lines) + "\n"


def guard_dir(run_dir: str, grants) -> str:
    """A directory of refusing shims, first on the child's PATH.

    Written per run rather than shipped, because what is refused depends on the grant
    this run was given, and a shipped guard would have to read state to know it -
    which is a second place the grant would live.

    The one thing the shims read at call time is this run's pending question, because
    that is the one thing that changes while the run is going.
    """
    guard = os.path.join(run_dir, "guard")
    os.makedirs(guard, exist_ok=True)
    path = clean_path()
    question = os.path.join(run_dir, "question.json")
    for name, clauses in GUARDS.items():
        body = shim(name, clauses, shutil.which(name, path=path), grants, question)
        target = os.path.join(guard, name)
        with open(target, "w") as fh:
            fh.write(body)
        os.chmod(target, 0o755)
    return guard


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The runbook. Durable gotchas, and nothing else may reach it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

RUNBOOK_HEAD = """# Cleanup runbook

What a cleanup run learned that it could not have worked out from the fleet alone,
and what SIANA answered when it asked. The cleaner reads this at the start of every
run, so a gotcha resolved once is not asked about twice.

Nothing writes here by hand and nothing writes here from a transcript. Entries are
appended by `siana-clean answer`, out of exactly two strings: the question a cleaner
wrote down before it stopped, and the answer SIANA recorded. So a guess cannot land
here, and neither can a stream fragment, a secret a tool printed, or anything else
that was only ever in a private context.

Each entry carries the id of the question it came from. Appending the same one twice
is a no-op, which is what makes a retried answer safe.
"""


def runbook_init(path: str) -> None:
    """Create it if it is not there, and never touch it if it is. Later runs append
    to it and the captain reads it, so a rewrite would lose the fleet's accumulated
    answers in order to restore a header nobody needed."""
    if os.path.isfile(path):
        return
    with open(path, "w") as fh:
        fh.write(RUNBOOK_HEAD)


def runbook_has(path: str, qid: str) -> bool:
    try:
        with open(path) as fh:
            return f"<!-- siana-clean:{qid} -->" in fh.read()
    except OSError:
        return False


def runbook_append(path: str, qid: str, question: str, answer: str) -> bool:
    """Append one resolved gotcha, once.

    Idempotent by the question's id and not by its text: two runs can legitimately
    reach the same wording about different worktrees, and de-duplicating on prose
    would silently drop the second. Returns whether anything was written, so the
    caller can say so rather than guess.

    The two strings are passed through as they were recorded and are never
    reformatted or summarised. An entry that has been through a summariser is no
    longer the answer SIANA gave, and this file's whole value is that it is.
    """
    if runbook_has(path, qid):
        return False
    runbook_init(path)
    # One open-append-close, so a second process appending at the same moment cannot
    # interleave inside an entry.
    with open(path, "a") as fh:
        fh.write(f"\n<!-- siana-clean:{qid} -->\n"
                 f"## {question.strip()}\n\n{answer.strip()}\n")
    return True


def captain_decided(home: str, oid: str) -> dict:
    """The obligation a captain's answer was recorded under, or a refusal.

    Checked rather than taken on trust, because "an answer given here can never
    manufacture captain authority" is the design guarantee and an id nobody verified
    makes it a spelling convention. Before this, `--captain-decided anything`
    unblocked a captain-kind question.

    Three things have to hold, and each is a different way of being wrong: the
    obligation exists, it is a `decision` rather than a promise SIANA made to itself,
    and it is closed with an answer. An open one is a captain who has not spoken yet.

    Folded here rather than asked of `siana-owe`, because this is a read of an
    append-only store and the fold is the same three lines every reader in this fleet
    writes. Nothing here writes to it: closing an obligation stays `siana-owe`'s, so
    no cleanup run can retire a decision by answering a cleaner.
    """
    store = os.path.join(home, "obligations.jsonl")
    if not os.path.isfile(os.path.join(home, "schema-obligations.yaml")):
        die("this home has no obligations contract",
            "a captain's decision is recorded there, and without it there is no",
            "way to check that one was", "run `just upgrade` in the distro")
    records = {}
    try:
        with open(store) as fh:
            for line in fh:
                line = line.strip()
                if not line:
                    continue
                rec = json.loads(line)
                rid = rec.get("id")
                if rid is None:
                    continue
                if rec.get("_deleted"):
                    records.pop(rid, None)
                else:
                    records[rid] = rec
    except FileNotFoundError:
        pass
    except (OSError, json.JSONDecodeError) as exc:
        die(f"cannot read {store}: {exc}",
            "run `datafile -f obligations.jsonl repair` to quarantine the bad line")
    rec = records.get(oid)
    if rec is None:
        die(f"no obligation under that id: {oid}",
            "`siana-owe` lists what is open; record the captain's decision there",
            "first, and name it here once they have answered")
    if rec.get("kind") != "decision":
        die(f"{oid} is a {rec.get('kind')}, not a decision",
            "only a decision the captain answered can unblock a captain question")
    if rec.get("status") != "closed" or not rec.get("answer"):
        die(f"{oid} is still open: the captain has not answered it",
            f"  {rec.get('body', '')}".rstrip(),
            f"close it with what they said: siana-owe close {oid} --answer <text>",
            "answering here first would be manufacturing the authority this",
            "whole path exists to require")
    return rec


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Reading a run's state, with liveness folded in.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

def state(paths: Home, run_id: str) -> dict:
    """The run record, with `running` re-derived rather than believed.

    A stored status is what a process last managed to write, and a process killed
    between spawning a child and recording its exit never wrote anything. So the one
    status that could be a lie is checked against the operating system every time it
    is read, exactly as `siana-watch` and `siana-afk` check their grants.

    Identity is the run id in the child's argument list, and not the command string
    recorded when it was spawned. That string is captured the instant after `Popen`
    returns, which is before the child has finished exec'ing: a `#!/usr/bin/env`
    shebang means `ps` first reports `/usr/bin/env python3 ...` and then reports
    `python3 ...`, so an exact comparison failed intermittently and a live run read as
    interrupted. `abort` then took its "the process is already gone" branch and never
    killed the cleaner, which is how a run could be aborted with the cleaner still
    working. The run id is unique, appears in the child's argv because its system
    prompt is written under the run directory, and survives an exec unchanged.
    """
    rec = read_json(paths.record(run_id), "run record")
    if rec is None:
        known = ", ".join(paths.ids()) or "(there are none)"
        die(f"no cleanup run under that id: {run_id}", f"runs: {known}")
    if rec.get("status") == "running":
        pid = rec.get("pid")
        if not (pid and run_id in process_command(pid)):
            rec = dict(rec, status="failed",
                       detail="the process running it is gone; it was interrupted")
    elif rec.get("status") == "starting":
        # The same lie by one step earlier. `starting` is written before the cleaner
        # is spawned, and a `start` that dies in between - no `pi` on PATH, a signal,
        # a killed terminal - never wrote anything again, so the record said a run
        # was about to begin for as long as the home existed. It blocked nothing, but
        # it accumulated in `status` and in the captain's report as work in flight
        # that no process was doing.
        #
        # Identity is the exact command rather than the run id in it: the creating
        # process is `siana-clean start`, which does not name the run it is about to
        # make, and it has finished exec'ing before the record is written. This is
        # the comparison `take_lock` makes of a lock holder, for the same reason.
        #
        # Terminal and never resumed. Nothing is started here on the strength of a
        # record: a run whose creator died before spawning has no cleaner to adopt,
        # and `start` is what begins one.
        pid, command = rec.get("pid"), rec.get("command") or ""
        if not (pid and command and process_command(pid) == command):
            rec = dict(rec, status="failed",
                       detail="the process starting it is gone; no cleaner ran")
    return rec


def pending(paths: Home, run_id: str):
    """The question this run stopped on, or None.

    Its own file, and it exists only while the question is unanswered: `answer` moves
    it into the answers log and removes it. So the presence of that one file is the
    whole of "a question is waiting", which is what makes the state legible after a
    restart without anything having to be reconciled.
    """
    return read_json(paths.question(run_id), "pending question")


def answers(paths: Home, run_id: str):
    """Every question this run has asked and had answered, oldest first."""
    out = []
    try:
        with open(paths.answers(run_id)) as fh:
            for line in fh:
                line = line.strip()
                if line:
                    try:
                        out.append(json.loads(line))
                    except json.JSONDecodeError:
                        continue
    except OSError:
        pass
    return out


def rounds(paths: Home, run_id: str):
    """Every round this run has already done, oldest first. Append-only, so the file
    is the history and nothing has to be recomputed from a transcript."""
    out = []
    try:
        with open(paths.log(run_id)) as fh:
            for line in fh:
                line = line.strip()
                if line:
                    try:
                        out.append(json.loads(line))
                    except json.JSONDecodeError:
                        continue
    except OSError:
        pass
    return out


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Running one round.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

def cleaner_prompt() -> str:
    """The cleaner's definition, from the package this command ships beside.

    Found by this file's own resolved location rather than by configuration. The
    command is symlinked onto the captain's PATH by `just init`, so the link is
    followed first; `SIANA_DISTRO` would be a second answer to the same question, and
    a home whose config had drifted would start a cleaner out of a distro that is no
    longer there.
    """
    distro = os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__)))
    path = os.path.join(distro, "template", "pi-siana", "agents", "cleaner.md")
    try:
        with open(path) as fh:
            return fh.read()
    except OSError:
        die(f"no cleaner definition at {path}",
            "it is what a cleanup run is; without it there is nothing to start",
            "reinstall the distro, or run `just init` in it")


def brief(paths: Home, rec: dict, resolved) -> str:
    """What the child is told, and the whole of what it carries forward.

    Never the previous round's transcript. A resumed cleaner gets the runbook, its
    own reports, and the answer it was waiting on - what it concluded, rather than
    everything it read to conclude it. That is the property keeping this out of
    SIANA's context and out of the child's alike: the state is small because it is
    the findings, and the findings are the only durable thing a round produces.
    """
    parts = [
        f"Cleanup run {rec['id']}.",
        "",
        f"Grant: {', '.join(rec['grants'])}.",
        f"Scope: {rec.get('project') or 'every project in the registry'}.",
        f"Runbook: {paths.runbook}",
        f"Ask with: siana-clean ask --run {rec['id']} --body <question>"
        " --kind <siana|captain>",
        "",
    ]
    try:
        with open(paths.runbook) as fh:
            parts += ["## The runbook, as it stands", "", fh.read().strip(), ""]
    except OSError:
        parts += ["## The runbook", "", "It is empty; this is the first run.", ""]
    history = rounds(paths, rec["id"])
    if history:
        parts += ["## What you have already established", ""]
        for r in history:
            parts += [f"### Round {r['round']}", "",
                      r.get("report") or "(the round produced no report)", ""]
    if resolved:
        parts += [
            "## The question you stopped on, and SIANA's answer",
            "",
            f"You asked: {resolved['body']}",
            "",
            f"SIANA answered: {resolved['answer']}",
            "",
            "That answer is in the runbook now. Carry on from exactly there.",
        ]
    return "\n".join(parts)


def assistant_text(line: str):
    """The text of an assistant message in pi's JSON stream, or None.

    Anything that is not a complete JSON object of the shape expected is skipped
    rather than reported. The stream is a machine format, and a partial line at the
    end of a killed process is the ordinary way it ends, so treating malformed output
    as a fault would turn every abort into an error about the wrong thing. What
    matters is that nothing here ever *acts* on the stream: the only thing read out
    of it is the round's report, and a round whose report is empty is reported empty.
    """
    try:
        event = json.loads(line)
    except (json.JSONDecodeError, TypeError, ValueError):
        return None
    if not isinstance(event, dict) or event.get("type") != "message_end":
        return None
    message = event.get("message")
    if not isinstance(message, dict) or message.get("role") != "assistant":
        return None
    content = message.get("content")
    if isinstance(content, str):
        return content or None
    if not isinstance(content, list):
        return None
    text = "".join(b.get("text", "") for b in content
                   if isinstance(b, dict) and b.get("type") == "text")
    return text or None


def tail(text: str, limit: int = 2000) -> str:
    text = (text or "").strip()
    return text if len(text) <= limit else "..." + text[-limit:]


def reap(proc) -> None:
    """Kill the child's whole process group, and make sure it is gone.

    The group rather than the pid, because pi spawns tools and a SIGTERM to the
    parent alone leaves a `git` or a `herdr` behind. SIGKILL after a grace period,
    because a child that will not take SIGTERM is exactly the wedged one this exists
    for.
    """
    for sig, wait in ((signal.SIGTERM, GRACE_S), (signal.SIGKILL, GRACE_S)):
        try:
            os.killpg(os.getpgid(proc.pid), sig)
        except (ProcessLookupError, PermissionError, OSError):
            return
        try:
            proc.wait(timeout=wait)
            return
        except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
            continue


def spawn(paths: Home, rec: dict, resolved) -> dict:
    """Run one round, and return what it produced.

    The child gets its own process group, so an abort or a timeout reaches whatever
    it started rather than only the process this holds a handle to. pi is resolved
    from the parent's PATH before the guard goes on the child's, because the guard
    refuses `pi` and a run that looked itself up through it could never start.
    """
    run_id = rec["id"]
    round_no = len(rounds(paths, run_id)) + 1
    pi = shutil.which("pi", path=clean_path())
    if pi is None:
        die("pi is not on PATH",
            "a cleanup run is a pi agent in its own context; without pi there is",
            "nothing to run it in",
            "install pi, or do this cleanup by hand with `siana-retire`")
    guard = guard_dir(paths.run_dir(run_id), rec["grants"])
    system = os.path.join(paths.run_dir(run_id), "cleaner.md")
    with open(system, "w") as fh:
        fh.write(cleaner_prompt())
    env = dict(os.environ)
    env["PATH"] = os.pathsep.join([guard, clean_path()])
    env["SIANA_HOME"] = paths.home
    env["SIANA_CLEAN_RUN"] = run_id
    env["SIANA_CLEAN_GRANTS"] = ",".join(rec["grants"])
    # `tasks` finds `datafile` by walking PATH and then *imports the file it finds*
    # as a python module. With the guard in front, the file it finds is a `/bin/sh`
    # shim, `spec_from_file_location` returns None for it, and every `tasks` call in
    # the run dies on an AttributeError about a loader.
    #
    # Found by a live cleaner, which reported "the initial `tasks` invocation hit a
    # Python loader error" and then worked around it - so the cost of this was a
    # round that could not read the queue at all, reported as a footnote.
    #
    # Naming the real module is the fix, and it is the same one `just init` writes
    # into `siana.env` for the same reason. Resolved through the symlink, because
    # what has to be imported is the `.py`, and past the guard, because the whole
    # problem is what PATH answers.
    datafile = shutil.which("datafile", path=clean_path())
    if datafile:
        env["TASKS_DATAFILE"] = os.path.realpath(datafile)
    # A cleaner must never be mistaken for a minion. `siana-afk` refuses to activate
    # in a minion's environment by reading this, and a cleanup child inheriting one
    # would be carrying a task id that is not its.
    env.pop("SIANA_TASK_ID", None)
    # The child runs in the home, because that is where the stores are, and the home
    # is SIANA's own project directory. Two flags keep it from becoming SIANA:
    #
    #   --no-approve      the home holds `.pi/settings.json` and
    #                     `.pi/extensions/wake.ts`. A cleaner that loaded the wake
    #                     extension would consume the watcher's counter, and SIANA
    #                     would simply stop being woken - silently, because a taken
    #                     wake looks exactly like a delivered one.
    #   -nc               the home holds `AGENTS.md`, which is SIANA's instructions.
    #                     A cleaner that read it would believe it was SIANA, and the
    #                     first thing that file tells SIANA is how to dispatch.
    #
    # Its own definition arrives by `--append-system-prompt` instead, which is
    # deliberate rather than incidental: a project-local prompt is trusted because
    # somebody approved the directory, and this one is trusted because this command
    # read it out of the distro it ships in and handed it over by path.
    # `-xt write,edit` because a cleaner writes nothing. It enumerates and delegates,
    # and the two tools it has no use for are the two that reach the runbook - the
    # one file whose whole value is that only a recorded answer lands in it. This
    # narrows that opening rather than closing it: a shell redirection still reaches
    # any file the user can write, exactly as an absolute path still gets past the
    # PATH guard, and the documentation says so in both places rather than claiming
    # a sandbox neither of them is.
    argv = [pi, "--mode", "json", "-p", "--no-session", "--no-approve", "-nc",
            "-xt", "write,edit",
            "--append-system-prompt", system, brief(paths, rec, resolved)]

    try:
        proc = subprocess.Popen(argv, cwd=paths.home, env=env,
                                stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.PIPE,
                                text=True, start_new_session=True)
    except OSError as exc:
        return {"round": round_no, "ended": now(), "exit": -1, "report": "",
                "failed": f"cannot start pi: {exc}", "stderr": "",
                "truncated": False}
    write_json(paths.record(run_id),
               dict(rec, status="running", pid=proc.pid,
                    command=process_command(proc.pid), round=round_no,
                    updated=now(), detail="a cleaner is working"))

    # stderr is drained on its own thread and into a bounded buffer. Both halves
    # matter. Left unread until after the stdout loop, a child that writes more than
    # the pipe buffer - 64KB on darwin - blocks in `write`, never exits, never closes
    # stdout, and the parent sits in `for line in proc.stdout` with nothing coming:
    # the round then burns its whole 30-minute budget and reports as a wedged
    # cleaner rather than as this harness deadlocking on its own pipe. And read
    # without a bound, a child that streams a gigabyte of tracebacks would be held
    # in memory whole, when what the failure report prints is the last two thousand
    # characters of it.
    errbuf = []

    def drain():
        for chunk in iter(lambda: proc.stderr.read(4096), ""):
            errbuf.append(chunk)
            if len(errbuf) > 8:
                del errbuf[:len(errbuf) - 8]

    report, truncated, written = "", False, 0
    # The deadline is a timer on the child's process group and not a check inside the
    # read loop. A wedged round is exactly one that has stopped producing output, so
    # a check that only runs when a line arrives never runs at all: the first version
    # of this waited the full two minutes of a `sleep 120` with a one-second bound
    # set. Killing the group is what ends the read, because it is what closes stdout.
    killed = []

    def on_deadline():
        killed.append(f"the round passed {duration(ROUND_S)}")
        try:
            os.killpg(os.getpgid(proc.pid), signal.SIGTERM)
        except (ProcessLookupError, PermissionError, OSError):
            pass

    def on_signal(signum, _frame):
        killed.append(f"cancelled by signal {signum}")
        try:
            os.killpg(os.getpgid(proc.pid), signal.SIGTERM)
        except (ProcessLookupError, PermissionError, OSError):
            pass

    # The cleaner is in its own session, so a signal sent to *this* process reaches
    # nothing but this process. Without these handlers a cancelled or timed-out call
    # killed the command and left the cleaner running as an orphan - still holding
    # its grant, still able to call `siana-retire` through the guard, with nothing
    # watching it and no round ever recorded. Python installs no SIGTERM handler, so
    # the `finally` below did not run either: the lock stayed taken.
    #
    # Handled rather than defaulted, so a cancel does what it is asked: kill the
    # cleaner, let the read loop end on the closed pipe, record the round, release
    # the lock. The exit is then an ordinary failed round rather than a death.
    previous = {}
    for sig in (signal.SIGTERM, signal.SIGINT):
        try:
            previous[sig] = signal.signal(sig, on_signal)
        except ValueError:
            pass                       # not the main thread; nothing to restore

    watchdog = threading.Timer(ROUND_S, on_deadline)
    watchdog.daemon = True
    watchdog.start()
    errors = threading.Thread(target=drain, daemon=True)
    errors.start()
    try:
        with open(paths.stream(run_id, round_no), "w") as out:
            for line in proc.stdout:
                if written < STREAM_CAP:
                    out.write(line)
                    written += len(line)
                elif not truncated:
                    truncated = True
                    out.write('{"type":"siana-clean","note":"stream cap reached"}\n')
                text = assistant_text(line)
                if text:
                    report = text
    except KeyboardInterrupt:
        killed.append("interrupted")
        raise
    finally:
        for sig, handler in previous.items():
            signal.signal(sig, handler)
        watchdog.cancel()
        if killed:
            reap(proc)
        code = proc.wait()
        # The child is gone, so the drain has reached end of file. Joined with a
        # bound anyway: this must never be the thing that hangs the command.
        errors.join(timeout=GRACE_S)
        stderr = "".join(errbuf)
    why = killed[0] if killed else None
    return {"round": round_no, "ended": now(), "exit": -1 if why else code,
            "report": report[:REPORT_CAP], "stderr": tail(stderr),
            "truncated": truncated,
            "failed": why or (None if code == 0 else f"the cleaner exited {code}")}


def finish(paths: Home, rec: dict, result: dict) -> int:
    """Record what a round did, and say what happens next.

    The round is logged before the run record is rewritten, so a crash between them
    loses the run's summary and never the round's evidence. The status is then
    derived from what is on disk rather than from what this believes: a question file
    that landed is a pending question whatever the child's exit code said, because
    the question was written first and deliberately.
    """
    run_id = rec["id"]
    with open(paths.log(run_id), "a") as fh:
        fh.write(json.dumps(result) + "\n")

    # The abort marker is read first, because the process that wrote it is not this
    # one. `abort` kills the cleaner while this is still blocked reading its output;
    # the pipe closes, the round ends as "the cleaner exited -15", and this used to
    # write that over the captain's stated reason - so `status`, the captain's report
    # and everything downstream said the cleaner had crashed rather than that it was
    # deliberately stopped. Derived from disk rather than from what this believes, the
    # way the pending question already is.
    stopped = read_json(paths.aborted(run_id), "abort record")
    question = pending(paths, run_id)
    if stopped:
        status, detail = "failed", f"aborted: {stopped['reason']}"
    elif question:
        status, detail = "question", question["body"]
    elif result["failed"]:
        status, detail = "failed", result["failed"]
    else:
        status, detail = "finished", "the round completed with nothing outstanding"
    rec = dict(rec, status=status, detail=detail, round=result["round"],
               updated=now())
    rec.pop("pid", None)
    rec.pop("command", None)
    write_json(paths.record(run_id), rec)

    print(f"run      {run_id}")
    print(f"  round  {result['round']}")
    if result["truncated"]:
        print("  note   the round's output hit its cap and was truncated")
    if status == "question":
        report_question(run_id, question)
        return 3
    if status == "failed":
        print(f"  failed {detail}")
        for line in result["stderr"].splitlines()[-6:]:
            print(f"    {line}")
        print("  state  nothing was left half-done. Every mutation this run could")
        print("         make goes through a command that fails closed on its own")
        print(f"  again  siana-clean start{scope_flags(rec)}")
        return 1
    print("  done   nothing outstanding")
    if result["report"]:
        print()
        print(result["report"])
    return 0


def report_question(run_id: str, question: dict) -> None:
    print(f"  asks   {question['body']}")
    print(f"  kind   {question['kind']}")
    for option in question.get("options", []):
        print(f"    option  {option}")
    if question["kind"] == "captain":
        print("  note   this one is the captain's. Record it as a decision:")
        print("         siana-owe decision <the question> --situation ... --option ...")
        print(f"  answer siana-clean answer {run_id} --text <what the captain said>"
              " --captain-decided <obligation id>")
    else:
        print(f"  answer siana-clean answer {run_id} --text <your answer>")


def scope_flags(rec: dict) -> str:
    flags = f" --project {rec['project']}" if rec.get("project") else ""
    for grant in rec["grants"]:
        if grant != "inventory":
            flags += f" --grant {grant}"
    return flags


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The subcommands.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

def need_home(paths: Home) -> None:
    """A home that is not there is a refusal, on every path that writes into one.

    `start` had this and `runbook` did not, so `siana-clean runbook` against a missing
    home died with a `FileNotFoundError` traceback. That path is reachable from
    SIANA's own context through the `siana_runbook` tool, which hands back whatever
    the command printed - so a traceback arrived where a recovery should have.
    """
    if not os.path.isdir(paths.home):
        die(f"no SIANA home at {paths.home}", "run `just init` in the distro")


def cmd_start(paths: Home, args) -> int:
    grants = ["inventory"] + [g for g in args.grant if g != "inventory"]
    for grant in grants:
        if grant not in GRANTS:
            die(f"no such grant: {grant}", f"the grants are: {', '.join(GRANTS)}")
    need_home(paths)

    take_lock(paths, "start")
    try:
        # One live run at a time, decided after the lock so that two `start`s racing
        # cannot both find the fleet quiet. A run holding an unanswered question is
        # live: starting a second would put two cleaners on one fleet with one of
        # them believing a question is still open.
        for existing in paths.ids():
            rec = state(paths, existing)
            if rec["status"] in ("running", "question", "answered"):
                die(f"cleanup run {existing} is still {rec['status']}",
                    f"  {rec.get('detail', '')}".rstrip(),
                    "one run at a time; finish or abort that one first",
                    f"`siana-clean status {existing}` says what it is doing")

        run_id = run_id_for(paths)
        os.makedirs(paths.run_dir(run_id), exist_ok=True)
        runbook_init(paths.runbook)
        # The creator's own pid and command go in, so that `state` can tell a run
        # that is about to start from one whose starter died before it could.
        rec = {"id": run_id, "started": now(), "updated": now(),
               "status": "starting", "grants": grants, "project": args.project,
               "round": 0, "detail": "not started yet",
               "pid": os.getpid(), "command": process_command(os.getpid())}
        write_json(paths.record(run_id), rec)
        print(f"started  {run_id}")
        return finish(paths, rec, spawn(paths, rec, None))
    finally:
        drop_lock(paths)


def cmd_resume(paths: Home, args) -> int:
    take_lock(paths, "resume")
    try:
        rec = state(paths, args.run)
        question = pending(paths, args.run)
        if question:
            die(f"{args.run} is still waiting for an answer",
                f"  {question['body']}",
                f"answer it first: siana-clean answer {args.run} --text <answer>")
        if rec["status"] == "running":
            die(f"{args.run} is already running (pid {rec.get('pid')})",
                "wait for it, or `siana-clean abort` it")
        # An abort ends a run. Resuming one would re-run a round under a marker that
        # says it was stopped, so every round after it would be recorded as the abort
        # rather than as what it did.
        stopped = read_json(paths.aborted(args.run), "abort record")
        if stopped:
            die(f"{args.run} was aborted: {stopped['reason']}",
                "an abort ends a run; its rounds and questions are kept as history",
                f"start a fresh one: siana-clean start{scope_flags(rec)}")
        history = answers(paths, args.run)
        if not history:
            die(f"{args.run} has no answered question to carry on from",
                f"it is {rec['status']}: {rec.get('detail', '')}".rstrip(),
                "a run resumes from an answer and from nothing else; start a new",
                f"one instead: siana-clean start{scope_flags(rec)}")
        resolved = history[-1]
        # The mark that this answer has been carried forward. Without it a second
        # `resume` would re-run the same round off the same answer, which is the
        # duplicate-delivery failure: the answers log is append-only and says nothing
        # about which of its entries a round has already been given.
        if rec.get("carried") == resolved["qid"]:
            die(f"{args.run} has already carried {resolved['qid']} forward",
                f"it is {rec['status']}: {rec.get('detail', '')}".rstrip(),
                "resuming again would re-run a round off an answer already used",
                f"start a fresh run instead: siana-clean start{scope_flags(rec)}")
        result = spawn(paths, rec, resolved)
        # Marked after the round, and only when the round actually ran. Written
        # before it, a resume that never started - no pi on PATH, a child that died
        # at once - left the mark set with the answer never delivered, and every
        # later resume refused on it. The run was then unrecoverable, and said so in
        # a refusal that named no recovery.
        #
        # A failed round is left unmarked deliberately: retrying it is the recovery,
        # and it cannot be a duplicate delivery because nothing was delivered.
        if result["failed"] is None:
            rec = dict(rec, carried=resolved["qid"])
        return finish(paths, rec, result)
    finally:
        drop_lock(paths)


def cmd_ask(paths: Home, args) -> int:
    """The cleaner's own call, and the only write it makes.

    Refuses a second question while one is open, so a cleaner that misread its
    instructions and kept going cannot overwrite the question SIANA is about to be
    shown.
    """
    run_id = args.run
    if not os.path.isdir(paths.run_dir(run_id)):
        die(f"no cleanup run under that id: {run_id}")
    open_question = pending(paths, run_id)
    if open_question:
        die(f"{run_id} already has a question waiting",
            f"  {open_question['body']}",
            "one at a time: stop now and let SIANA answer that one")
    body = (args.body or "").strip()
    if not body:
        die("a question needs a body", "what is it you cannot settle?")
    if args.kind not in ("siana", "captain"):
        die(f"no such question kind: {args.kind}",
            "`siana` is something this fleet's own rules can settle",
            "`captain` is a product choice or an irreversible action, and SIANA",
            "records those as decisions rather than answering them")
    slug = re.sub(r"[^a-z0-9]+", "-", body.lower())[:32].strip("-") or "question"
    # The run id is part of the question id, and that is not decoration. The runbook
    # is idempotent on this string, so a per-run counter alone means every run's
    # first question is `q1-<slug>` and two runs whose first questions share a
    # 32-character prefix collide: the second answer is silently never appended, and
    # the next cleaner reads the first run's answer as the fleet's policy. The run id
    # is already unique by construction, so prefixing with it closes that.
    qid = f"{run_id}-q{len(answers(paths, run_id)) + 1}-{slug}"
    write_json(paths.question(run_id),
               {"qid": qid, "run": run_id, "body": body, "kind": args.kind,
                "options": [o for o in args.option if o.strip()], "asked": now()})
    print(f"asked    {qid}")
    print("  stop here. SIANA answers it and resumes this run.")
    return 0


def cmd_answer(paths: Home, args) -> int:
    rec = state(paths, args.run)
    question = pending(paths, args.run)
    if question is None:
        history = answers(paths, args.run)
        if history:
            die(f"{args.run} is not waiting on a question",
                f"the last one, {history[-1]['qid']}, was answered:"
                f" {history[-1]['answer']}",
                f"resume it: siana-clean resume {args.run}")
        die(f"{args.run} is not waiting on a question",
            f"it is {rec['status']}: {rec.get('detail', '')}".rstrip())
    text = (args.text or "").strip()
    if not text:
        die(f"what is the answer to {question['qid']}?",
            f"  {question['body']}",
            "usage: siana-clean answer <run> --text <answer>")
    decision = None
    if question["kind"] == "captain":
        if not args.captain_decided:
            die("this question is the captain's, not SIANA's",
                f"  {question['body']}",
                "record it as an ordinary decision and wait for the captain:",
                "  siana-owe decision <the question> --situation ... --option ...",
                "then answer here with --captain-decided <obligation id> once they",
                "have said. An answer given here cannot make captain authority; it",
                "only unblocks a cleaner already told what the captain decided")
        decision = captain_decided(paths.home, args.captain_decided)

    resolved = dict(question, answer=text, answered=now())
    if decision is not None:
        resolved["decision"] = args.captain_decided
    # The answer lands in three places and the order is the whole of its safety. The
    # append-only log first, because that is what `resume` reads and what survives a
    # restart. The runbook second, idempotently, so a retry after a crash between the
    # two writes it rather than skipping it. The pending question is removed last:
    # while it is there the run is still unanswered, so every earlier step can be
    # repeated and none of them can be half-done.
    with open(paths.answers(args.run), "a") as fh:
        fh.write(json.dumps(resolved) + "\n")
    added = runbook_append(paths.runbook, resolved["qid"], resolved["body"], text)
    os.unlink(paths.question(args.run))
    write_json(paths.record(args.run),
               dict(rec, status="answered", updated=now(),
                    detail=f"answered {resolved['qid']}; resume to carry on"))
    print(f"answered {resolved['qid']}")
    if decision is not None:
        print(f"  captain {decision['answer']}")
        print(f"    from {args.captain_decided}")
    print(f"  runbook {'written' if added else 'already had this one'}")
    print(f"  resume  siana-clean resume {args.run}")
    return 0


def cmd_abort(paths: Home, args) -> int:
    rec = state(paths, args.run)
    # Written before the kill, and that order is the whole of it. The process running
    # the round is a different one and is about to notice its child die; if it reached
    # `finish` before this landed, it would record its own account of the death and
    # the reason for stopping would be gone.
    write_json(paths.aborted(args.run), {"reason": args.reason, "at": now()})
    if rec["status"] == "running":
        try:
            os.killpg(os.getpgid(rec["pid"]), signal.SIGTERM)
        except (ProcessLookupError, PermissionError, OSError, TypeError):
            pass
    # A question on an aborted run is history, and it has to stop being a pending
    # one. `pending()` is "the file is there", so a kept question.json meant `status`
    # went on exiting 3 forever, the captain's report went on listing it under what
    # the captain has to decide, and the hint `status` prints made it worse: an
    # answer would have moved the run to `answered`, and `start` refuses every new
    # run while one is. Moved rather than deleted, because what the run asked is the
    # useful half of an aborted run.
    question = pending(paths, args.run)
    if question:
        os.replace(paths.question(args.run),
                   os.path.join(paths.run_dir(args.run),
                                f"abandoned-{question['qid']}.json"))
    write_json(paths.record(args.run),
               dict(rec, status="failed", updated=now(),
                    detail=f"aborted: {args.reason}"))
    print(f"aborted  {args.run}")
    print(f"  why    {args.reason}")
    print("  state  the run's rounds are kept and nothing was undone")
    if question:
        print(f"  unasked {question['body']}")
        print("          it was never answered; a new run will ask again if it")
        print("          still matters")
    return 1


def cmd_status(paths: Home, args) -> int:
    ids = [args.run] if args.run else paths.ids()
    records = []
    for run_id in ids:
        rec = dict(state(paths, run_id))
        rec["question"] = pending(paths, run_id)
        rec["answers"] = answers(paths, run_id)
        rec["rounds"] = len(rounds(paths, run_id))
        records.append(rec)
    if args.as_json:
        print(json.dumps(records, indent=2))
        return 3 if any(r["question"] for r in records) else 0
    if not records:
        print("cleanup  no runs")
        return 0
    for rec in records:
        print(f"run      {rec['id']}  {rec['status']}")
        print(f"  grant  {', '.join(rec['grants'])}")
        print(f"  scope  {rec.get('project') or 'every project'}")
        print(f"  rounds {rec['rounds']}")
        if rec.get("detail"):
            print(f"  is     {rec['detail']}")
        if rec["question"]:
            report_question(rec["id"], rec["question"])
    return 3 if any(r["question"] for r in records) else 0


def cmd_runbook(paths: Home, args) -> int:
    need_home(paths)
    runbook_init(paths.runbook)
    with open(paths.runbook) as fh:
        sys.stdout.write(fh.read())
    return 0


def main() -> int:
    home = os.environ.get("SIANA_HOME", os.path.expanduser("~/.siana"))
    paths = Home(home)

    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        prog="siana-clean", description=__doc__,
        formatter_class=argparse.RawDescriptionHelpFormatter)
    sub = ap.add_subparsers(dest="cmd")
    s = sub.add_parser("start")
    s.add_argument("--project", help="one project handle; omitted means all of them")
    s.add_argument("--grant", action="append", default=[],
                   help=f"one of: {', '.join(GRANTS)}. Repeatable")
    st = sub.add_parser("status")
    st.add_argument("run", nargs="?")
    st.add_argument("--json", action="store_true", dest="as_json")
    a = sub.add_parser("answer")
    a.add_argument("run")
    a.add_argument("--text", help="the answer, in the words the runbook will keep")
    a.add_argument("--captain-decided", metavar="OBLIGATION",
                   help="the obligation id the captain's answer was recorded under")
    r = sub.add_parser("resume")
    r.add_argument("run")
    ab = sub.add_parser("abort")
    ab.add_argument("run")
    ab.add_argument("--reason", required=True)
    sub.add_parser("runbook")
    k = sub.add_parser("ask")
    k.add_argument("--run", required=True)
    k.add_argument("--body", required=True)
    k.add_argument("--kind", default="siana")
    k.add_argument("--option", action="append", default=[])
    args = ap.parse_args()

    if args.cmd is None:
        args = ap.parse_args(["status"])
    return {"start": cmd_start, "status": cmd_status, "resume": cmd_resume,
            "ask": cmd_ask, "answer": cmd_answer, "abort": cmd_abort,
            "runbook": cmd_runbook}[args.cmd](paths, args)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    try:
        sys.exit(main())
    except Refusal as r:
        print(f"error: {r.message}", file=sys.stderr)
        for h in r.hints:
            print(f"  {h}", file=sys.stderr)
        raise
    except KeyboardInterrupt:
        sys.exit(130)
